course: technology
lesson: 9
title: Phones and computer chips
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Inside your phone is a computer chip smaller than a fingernail holding billions of tiny switches, printed with light onto a thin slice of silicon, which comes from sand made extremely pure.
explain: Chips are made in clean rooms with far less dust in the air than a hospital operating room, and workers wear full "bunny suits", because one speck of dust can ruin a chip.
explain: A phone is put together from parts made by many companies in many countries: screens, batteries, cameras, chips and cases meet in big assembly factories, many of them in China, India and Vietnam.
explain: Your phone also holds metals dug from mines around the world, such as lithium, cobalt, copper and gold, so an old phone in a drawer is a little treasure chest worth recycling.
explain: Phone batteries can catch fire if they are crushed or thrown in the household bin, so old phones go to proper recycling points.
fact: A computer chip has billions of tiny switches printed layer by layer with light onto very pure silicon; the newest chips are described in nanometres, billionths of a metre.
fact: Big chip makers include TSMC in Taiwan, Samsung and Intel.
fact: Chip clean rooms filter their air all the time, so there is far less dust than in a hospital operating room, and workers wear full bunny suits because one speck can ruin a chip.
fact: Silicon comes from sand made extremely pure; it is melted and a big crystal is slowly pulled out as a cylinder, then sliced into thin shiny discs called wafers.
fact: Smartphones are assembled from parts made by many companies, in big factories many of them in China, India and Vietnam, and one phone contains dozens of different chemical elements.
fact: A phone screen has tough glass over layers of tiny coloured lights and a see-through grid that senses where your finger is by a small electric change.
fact: Touch screens do not work with most gloves because they need the electricity of your skin; a cracked screen can cut fingers, so cover it until it is fixed.
fact: A circuit board is a green board with copper tracks; robots place tiny parts, tens of thousands an hour, and an oven melts solder, a metal that melts easily, to fix them.
fact: Phones need metals like lithium, cobalt, copper and gold from mines in many countries; some mines have been unsafe or used child labour, so companies are pushed to check their suppliers.
fact: A tonne of old phones holds more gold than a tonne of gold ore (the rock dug from a gold mine), though each single phone holds only a tiny bit.
fact: Phones are checked before sale: screens, cameras, buttons, speakers and batteries are tested, and in test labs some phones are dropped, bent and dunked in water on purpose.
fact: Old phones are taken apart, their batteries removed safely and their metals melted out and reused; phones and batteries must never go in the household bin because they can catch fire.
fact: Under each laptop key is a tiny switch on a thin layer of circuits; pressing it closes a contact and the computer reads which key it was, and spilled drinks can ruin a laptop.
fact: The QWERTY order of keys comes from typewriters of the 1870s; a popular story says it slowed typists so the keys would not jam, but historians still argue about the real reason.
exercise: What is a computer chip? | Billions of tiny switches printed onto pure silicon | A thin slice of potato with tiny wires pushed into it | A small battery that stores all of your photos and pictures | A magnet that remembers numbers
exercise: Which company is a big chip maker? | TSMC in Taiwan | Lego in Denmark | Ford in the USA | Cadbury in England
exercise: Where does the silicon for chips come from? | Sand made extremely pure | Melted-down glass bottles | Oil pumped from deep under the ground | Sap from rubber trees
exercise: What is a silicon wafer? | A thin shiny disc sliced from a big silicon crystal | A thin biscuit that the chip factory workers eat | A sheet of foil for wrapping finished chips | A plastic tray for carrying phones round the factory
exercise: Why do chip workers wear bunny suits? | One speck of dust can ruin a chip | To keep warm in the cold clean rooms | So the robots can tell who they are | To protect themselves from poisonous chips
exercise: How clean is the air in a chip clean room? | Far less dusty than a hospital operating room | About as dusty as an ordinary classroom | About as dusty as a garden on a windy day | It is never cleaned, because dust helps the chips work
exercise: Where are many smartphones assembled? | In big factories in China, India and Vietnam | By hand in the back room of each phone shop | In space, where there is no dust at all | In one single factory for the whole world
exercise: How does a touch screen know where your finger is? | A see-through grid senses a small electric change from your skin | A tiny camera under the glass watches your finger move | It feels the weight of your finger pressing on the glass | It hears the tap of your nail with a small microphone
exercise: Why don't most gloves work on a touch screen? | The screen needs the electricity of your skin | Gloves are too heavy for the thin glass to feel | Gloves are too warm, and the screen switches off | The screen thinks gloves are dirt
exercise: What is a circuit board? | A green board with copper tracks connecting the parts | A board game about how electricity works | A chopping board used in a chip factory | A noticeboard where factory workers pin up news
exercise: How are tiny parts fixed to a circuit board? | Robots place them and an oven melts solder | Workers glue each one down with a glue stick | They are sewn on with very fine wire | Magnets hold them in place for ever
exercise: Which metals does a phone need? | Lithium, cobalt, copper and gold | Only iron, like a frying pan | No metals at all, only plastic and glass | Only lead and mercury
exercise: Why are phone companies pushed to check the mines they buy from? | Some mines have been unsafe or used child labour | Mines might send metal of the wrong colour or size | Many of the mines are on the Moon | Miners might keep the finished phones for themselves
exercise: Which holds more gold: a tonne of old phones or a tonne of gold ore? | A tonne of old phones | A tonne of gold ore | Both hold exactly the same amount | Neither holds any gold at all
exercise: How do phone makers test whether a phone is waterproof? | They dunk it in water | They ask the phone questions | They leave it out in the hot sun | They weigh it before and after
exercise: Why must old phones never go in the household bin? | Their batteries can catch fire | They are too heavy for the bin lorries | They might ring and buzz inside the bin | Bin crews would read the messages
exercise: What happens when you press a laptop key? | A tiny switch closes a contact and the computer reads which key | A little hammer inside hits the back of the screen | A spring throws the letter up onto the screen | The key lights up the letter all by itself
exercise: Where does the QWERTY order of keys come from? | Typewriters of the 1870s | The first mobile phones | A spelling rule about putting vowels first | Computer games of the 1980s
test: «chips are made of sand?? lol no way» What is right? | Silicon chips start from sand that is purified and grown into crystals | No, computer chips are made of thin slices of potato | No, chips are made of plastic, like toy bricks | Yes, beach sand is printed on just as it comes
test: «screen's cracked but still works, I'll just keep swiping» What is the safe advice? | Cover the crack until it is fixed, as broken glass can cut | Keep swiping, because phone glass is made so that it can never cut you | Pick the loose bits of glass out with your nails | Lick the crack to seal it
test: «my old phone's just junk in the drawer» What is the clever answer? | It holds metals like gold and copper that recycling can reuse | It has no metal in it that is worth anything | Old phones slowly turn back into sand in a drawer | Phones left in a drawer slowly recharge themselves and work again
test: «chucked a dead phone battery in the kitchen bin, sorted» What is right? | Wrong: batteries in bins can catch fire, so take them to recycling | Fine: a dead battery has nothing left in it, so it can never burn | Fine, as long as the bin lid is shut tight | Right, batteries go with the food waste
test: What would happen if a speck of dust fell on a chip while it was being printed? | That chip could be ruined | The chip would become faster | The dust would turn into a new switch | Nothing, chips need a little dust
test: «gloves on, can't swipe, phone's broke» What is right? | The screen needs the electricity of skin, which most gloves block | The phone breaks in cold weather and needs fixing | Gloves are too slippery for the glass to feel | Touch screens only work in the summer months
test: «my bro says QWERTY's in that order to slow typists down, 100% fact» What is right? | That is one popular story, and historians still argue about it | Yes, it is proven beyond any doubt, and every typing book says so | No, the keys are really in alphabetical order | No, a computer chose the best order in 1990
test: A test lab dunks 1 phone in every 4. Out of 20 phones, how many get dunked? | 5 | 4 | 16 | 80
test: In a film one person builds a phone alone in a garage in a day. What is the truth for real phones? | Parts come from many countries and are put together in big factories | One clever person builds each phone alone from scratch | Phones are grown from seeds in special greenhouses | Phones are carved from a single block of metal
test: A laptop has 4 rows of 12 keys, no wait, 4 rows of 10. How many keys is that? | 40 | 48 | 14 | 44
test: «gold in phones, so I'll smash mine and get rich» What is right? | One phone holds only a tiny bit, and smashing it can start a battery fire | Each phone hides a small bar of gold inside, so smashing it open is worth it | Smashing a phone makes its gold shine out | Phone gold appears only if you burn it
test: «spilt me juice on the laptop, it's fine, it'll dry» What is the safer habit? | Keep drinks away from laptops, as spills can ruin them | Juice is good for the keys and makes them press more softly | Laptops are always waterproof, so a spill can't really hurt | Pour water on straight away to wash the juice off
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the environment within a semiconductor clean room? | The air is continually filtered, such that far less dust is present than in a hospital operating room | The air is deliberately charged with dust in order to protect the delicate chips | The room is left open to the outdoors so as to provide ample ventilation | The room is maintained at the temperature of an oven
exam: Identify the principal reason for which touch screens fail to respond to most gloved fingers. | The screen detects a small electrical change produced by the skin, which gloves obstruct | The screen responds solely to the warmth of a finger, and gloved fingers are invariably too cold | Gloves are too heavy for the screen to register | The screen responds only to the sound of a tap
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the disposal of mobile telephones. | They must not be placed in household waste, since their batteries may ignite | They may safely be placed with food waste for collection | They contain no recoverable materials of any value whatsoever | They are dissolved by water once flushed into the drain
exam: Determine the aggregate number of keys upon a keyboard possessing five rows of fourteen keys each. | Seventy | Nineteen | Fifty-six | Eighty-four
